      <p class="kicker">Lagom</p>
      <h1>Layers a reader can trust.</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The raw feed keeps every revision of an event. That is not what a question asks. dbt
          builds three layers. Bronze is what the load wrote. Silver,
          <code>stg_usgs__events</code>, is the latest revision, typed, times in UTC. Gold is
          <code>fct_earthquakes</code>, earthquakes only, with magnitude and depth classes, and
          <code>agg_earthquakes_daily_region</code>, counts by day and region. Both gold models
          have an enforced contract.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same build runs the tests: keys, ranges, accepted values, reconciliation of row
          counts from bronze to silver to gold to the daily aggregate, and unit tests of the
          deduplication, the region parsing and the class boundaries. Freshness of bronze is a
          separate check. The build does not run it, and the roadmap item that would declare and
          assert a threshold is still open.
        </p>
        <p>
          There were two homes for these models. A CronJob used to run them in Postgres. The
          scheduled job now runs them on the lake target. The production counts for both paths are
          on the USGS showcase, labelled as which path they belong to. The figure below is the CI
          gate, a fixture, not a production load.
        </p>
      </div>
